@*
    Hey B.

    A COPY OF WakeWordActivity: the mark at 200dp, the instruction, the quiet hint,
    then the two claims low on the screen.

    THE INSTRUCTION IS THE INTERFACE. Quotation marks around the phrase so it reads
    as something to say out loud rather than as a label - there is nothing to press
    on this screen, and that is the point of it.
*@
@page "/wake"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IWakeWord Wake
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="wake">

    @* The same mark as everywhere else, listening. EarView in the native screen is
       the same ring and arcs; reusing BrandMark keeps one drawing rather than a
       fifth copy of the shape. *@
    <div class="ear">
        <BrandMark Fluid="true" Halo="true" Ring="true"
                   State="@(_status.State == WakeState.Listening ? MarkState.Listening : MarkState.Idle)"
                   Level="@_flash" />
    </div>

    <div class="status">@_status.Status</div>
    <div class="hint">@_status.Hint</div>

    <div class="spacer"></div>

    @if (_status.State == WakeState.NeedsPermission)
    {
        @* PRESSED, AND THE CODE CAN NOW TELL. Arriving on this screen asks for the
           microphone too - quietly, because nobody pressed anything - and with one
           path for both, a phone that had refused permanently opened Circle AI
           Service's page in Settings on EVERY VISIT, unprompted. This button is the
           press that earns that. *@
        <button class="allow" @onclick="Press">@MicrophoneAsk.Action</button>
    }
    else if (_status.State == WakeState.NotInstalled)
    {
        @*
            THE INSTRUCTION, PRESSABLE. This state prints "turn on Waking under
            Settings › Phone" and left somebody to walk there - on the one screen
            in the app with nothing else to press, which makes a dead end look
            like a broken feature.
        *@
        <button class="allow" @onclick='() => Nav.NavigateTo("settings")'>Turn it on</button>
    }

    @*
        KEPT, NOT RECORDED, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE CLAIM.
        This said "nothing is recorded", which is not true and cannot be: audio
        that is never recorded cannot be transcribed, and the phone holds a
        turn's worth of samples for as long as it takes to read them. Saying
        otherwise puts a promise on screen that the code breaks by existing.
        What IS true is that none of it is kept and none of it leaves - and that
        is the thing somebody was worried about anyway. Nobody minds the phone
        hearing them; they mind it keeping a copy.
    *@
    @*
        AND "NOTHING IS KEPT" WAS STILL TOO BROAD, which is the correction the review
        of 2026-10-23 forced. The LISTENING keeps nothing - that is exactly what the
        code does. What you ASK it, once it has woken, is written to this phone's own
        store with its answer and purged after a week. Five screens made the broad
        claim; MicrophoneAsk owns both halves of the true one now, so the next
        correction happens in one place.
    *@
    <div class="claims">
        <div class="claim">@MicrophoneAsk.SoundNotKept</div>
        <div class="claim">@MicrophoneAsk.AsksKept</div>
    </div>

</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What is on screen before the listener has said anything.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// NO PHRASE UNTIL THE LISTENER NAMES ONE. This started as 'Say "Hey B"',
    /// hardcoded - so a phone set to Japanese opened this screen telling its
    /// owner to say an English phrase, until the first report replaced it. The
    /// wake phrase has one source and it is not a literal in a component.
    /// </remarks>
    WakeStatus _status = new(WakeState.Preparing, "Getting ready", "One moment…");
    CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    /// <summary>
    /// A brief pulse when the phrase lands, so the mark acknowledges being heard.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Driven by an actual detection, not by a timer. The mark's own rule: a fake
    /// level lies about the one thing the person is trying to find out.
    /// </remarks>
    double _flash;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => await Start();

    /// <summary>The button: a press, so a refusal may send them to Settings.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// THE ONE NON-QUIET ASK ON THIS SCREEN. ListenAsync asks on arrival as well -
    /// opening this screen is asking for the phone to listen - and the two were the
    /// same call, so the rule the whole mechanism rests on, that only a press earns a
    /// trip to Settings, was broken by the screen that asks most often.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Press()
    {
        await Wake.RequestMicrophoneAsync(quietly: false);
        await Start();
    }

    async Task Start()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

        var updates = new Progress<WakeStatus>(s =>
        {
            _status = s;

            // A PULSE THAT ENDS. This set the level to 1 on Heard and only reset
            // it on the NEXT report - and the listener does not report again after
            // waking, it goes straight back to capturing. So the mark stayed fully
            // scaled from the first detection onwards, which reads as a microphone
            // stuck open rather than as an acknowledgement.
            if (s.State == WakeState.Heard) Flash();
            else _flash = 0;

            InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });

        // Not awaited: ListenAsync runs until cancelled, and awaiting it here
        // would block the component's initialisation for the life of the screen.
        _ = Wake.ListenAsync(updates, _cts.Token);
    }

    /// <summary>Light the mark up, then let it settle.</summary>
    async void Flash()
    {
        _flash = 1;
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        await Task.Delay(600);
        _flash = 0;
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    async Task StopAsync()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;
        await _cts.CancelAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }

    /// <summary>Releases the microphone when the screen goes away.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A wake listener has no natural end, so leaving the screen has to be the
    /// end. Without this the microphone stays open behind whatever the person
    /// navigated to.
    /// </remarks>
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync() => await StopAsync();
}
